(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the two primary clinical priorities in stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Identifying the causative organism by culture and selecting an appropriate antibiotic","Determining the allergen and arranging patch testing","Staging the ulcer and measuring its depth","Distinguishing it from cellulitis, and determining whether compression therapy can be used safely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both are named as the priorities. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of pemphigus?","choices":["Loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion, called acantholysis, from autoantibodies to adhesion molecules, producing intraepithelial blisters","Autoimmune damage to the epithelial basement membrane producing subepithelial blistering that resolves without any scarring","A type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction to an external allergen","Impaired stratum-corneum hydration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a life-threatening blistering disorder of skin and mucous membranes. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"What does light microscopy show in bullous pemphigoid?","choices":["Acantholysis","Band-like infiltration of neutrophils throughout the papillary dermis","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae","Neutrophils aligned in a straight narrow row at the dermal-epidermal junction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy of a lesion goes for histopathology and perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"How is a macule defined in this lecture?","choices":["A flat elevated plateau lesion measuring greater than one centimetre across","A flat lesion less than one centimetre, without elevation or depression","A flat lesion less than five millimetres","An elevated solid lesion less than one centimetre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patch is the same lesion larger than one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is a positive Nikolsky sign?","choices":["Lesions develop at sites of trauma","Fine white lacy lines appearing across the surface of the affected plaques","The top layers of skin slip away or slough off from the lower layers when rubbed","Removal of scale produces pinpoint bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — positive in pemphigus, negative in bullous pemphigoid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"When should a lichen simplex chronicus plaque be biopsied?","choices":["When the patient reports that the itch is worse at night than by day","When emollients alone have failed to settle the plaque completely","When it is atypical, unilateral, nodular, ulcerated or treatment-resistant","When it has been present for longer than six weeks in any location"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the purpose is to exclude neoplasia or another inflammatory disorder. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 182"},{"q":"What are the four Ps of lichen planus?","choices":["Pruritic, pustular, patchy plaques","Purple, painless, peripheral pustular lesions","Pruritic, purple, polygonal, papules or plaques","Painful, pale, punched-out papules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — flat-topped, pink to violaceous and shiny, coalescing into larger plaques. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"What is a dermatoscope used for?","choices":["Magnified examination of a lesion's surface and pigment pattern","Rapid bedside identification of a mite and its eggs in oil","Detection of fungal elements after dissolving keratin in alkali","Measurement of the amount of viral genetic material present"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is the first-line tool for evaluating a pigmented lesion. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How do urushiol lesions present, and over what time course?","choices":["Diffuse urticarial wheals resolving within 24 hours","Greasy scaling patches in sebum-rich areas over several weeks","Intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions, erupting within 4 to 96 hours and persisting up to 3 weeks","Well-demarcated plaques shaped like the object contacted, erupting within minutes of exposure and clearing within a day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the linear pattern comes from grazing the plant in passing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which underlying process drives stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Autoimmune destruction of the epidermal basement membrane","Chronic venous disease with lower-leg oedema","Chronic arterial insufficiency with reduced perfusion","Repeated contact with an environmental allergen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the dermatitis is a consequence of venous hypertension. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"What is finasteride, and what are its cautions?","choices":["An oral antiandrogen used first line in women","A topical 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor applied to the crown, whose effects on libido and erectile function are permanent and do not reverse at all when the drug is stopped","An oral 5-alpha-reductase type 2 inhibitor; about 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, reversible on stopping, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy","A topical vasodilator effective mainly at the crown"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it works best combined with minoxidil. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"Which comorbidities are patients with psoriasis at increased risk of?","choices":["Cardiovascular events, type 2 diabetes mellitus, metabolic syndrome and lymphoma","Chronic kidney disease, hepatic cirrhosis and early-onset osteoporosis","Osteoporosis and vitamin D deficiency","Asthma and allergic rhinitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is a systemic inflammatory disease, not only a skin condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 155"},{"q":"What distinguishes a plaque from a patch?","choices":["A plaque is elevated and plateau-like; a patch is flat with no elevation","A plaque is larger than a patch","A plaque is fluid-filled and a patch is solid","A plaque is always poorly demarcated while a patch is always well demarcated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both exceed one centimetre, so elevation is the discriminator. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What distinguishes an ulceration from an erosion?","choices":["An ulceration is full-thickness loss extending to the dermis or deeper; an erosion loses only the epidermis","An ulceration is linear and an erosion is round","An ulceration is painful and an erosion is painless","An ulceration involves only the epidermis and always heals without scarring, while an erosion reaches the dermis or deeper"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — depth is the discriminator, and it determines whether the lesion can scar. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What proportion of children worldwide are affected by atopic dermatitis, and in which sex is it more common?","choices":["About 20% of children, more common in females","About 5% of children, more common in males","About 40% of children, with no sex difference","About 20% of children, more common in males"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It often begins in infancy or childhood; adult-onset disease occurs but is rare. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of allergic contact dermatitis, and roughly what proportion of people in the United States react to it?","choices":["Perfumes and fragrance mixes — approximately 60% of people","Urushiol sap from Toxicodendron species — approximately 50 to 75%","Nickel — approximately 90%","Neomycin — approximately 30%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which explains why not everyone who contacts poison ivy, sumac or oak reacts. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What underlies xerosis, and who is most affected?","choices":["Impaired stratum-corneum hydration promoted by ageing, low humidity, hot water, detergents, atopy and systemic disease, commonly affecting older adults especially in winter","Overgrowth of Malassezia in sebum-rich areas, in young men","Chronic venous hypertension in the lower legs, in older adults","An autoimmune process affecting the epithelial basement membrane, occurring most often in the elderly and remitting spontaneously after five or six years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, causing tightness, pruritus, rough scale, fissuring or eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 152"},{"q":"What is the most important modifiable association of perioral dermatitis?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid exposure","Sun exposure","Overgrowth of Malassezia yeast","Nickel exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and treatment begins by stopping it, with a warning that the eruption may temporarily worsen on withdrawal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which three agents make up the medium to high potency tier?","choices":["Betamethasone valerate 0.025%, tazarotene 0.1% and adapalene 0.1% preparations","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1%, betamethasone valerate 0.1% and betamethasone dipropionate 0.05%","Hydrocortisone 2.5%, betamethasone valerate 0.025% and clobetasol propionate 0.05%","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1%, hydrocortisone 1% and clobetasol propionate 0.05%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Betamethasone appears twice here, in two different salts. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which finding on a potassium hydroxide preparation supports candidal diaper dermatitis?","choices":["Branching septate hyphae only","Multinucleated giant cells","Mites, eggs or faecal pellets","Budding yeast or pseudohyphae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fold involvement with satellite lesions is what prompts the test. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"How does guttate psoriasis present, and what precedes it?","choices":["Sudden generalised pustules with fever over the trunk and extremities","Silvery plaques on the extensor elbows and knees","Erythematous patches with margins studded with subcorneal pustules, appearing typically during the third trimester of pregnancy","Raindrop-shaped erythematous papules and plaques in children, typically 2 to 3 weeks after a streptococcal infection or upper respiratory infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It often needs no treatment, though phototherapy and topical steroids may be used. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"Which exposure most commonly precipitates perioral dermatitis?","choices":["Repeated shaving of the beard area with a blade","Topical corticosteroid applied to the face","Prolonged immersion of the hands in water","Occlusion of the skin under a plaster cast"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — continued exposure perpetuates it, so the steroid must be stopped. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What does a biopsy show in lichen planus?","choices":["A band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with junctional separation","Granular immunoglobulin A deposits at the dermal papillae","Subepidermal separation with linear immunoglobulin G at the basement membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a classic descriptive phrase worth recognising on sight. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"How are nummular eczema plaques described?","choices":["Greasy yellow scaling plaques in sebum-rich areas","Violaceous flat-topped polygonal papules with fine white lines","Intensely pruritic, round coin-shaped, light pink, scaly, thin plaques 1 to 10 cm, uniform without central clearing","Well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with prominent silvery scale on the extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, most commonly on the extremities though they can occur on the trunk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What defines a wheal?","choices":["A fluid-filled lesion containing leukocytes","A fluid-filled lesion under one centimetre","A deposit of blood in the skin measuring one to two millimetres across","A firm oedematous plaque produced by infiltration of fluid into the dermis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the fluid is in the tissue rather than in a cavity, which is what separates it from a vesicle. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which supportive measure is described as one of the most important considerations in alopecia areata?","choices":["Referral for hair transplantation early in the disease course","Referral to a wig service before starting any medical therapy","Psychological support, with support groups offered","Referral to endocrinology for thyroid function assessment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the visible nature of the condition carries real psychological weight. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"How can atopic dermatitis appear differently across skin tones?","choices":["Violaceous on lighter skin, but hypopigmented on darker skin in every case","Angry and inflamed on lighter skin, but almost silvery on darker skin","Silvery on lighter skin, but angry and inflamed on darker skin","Identical on all skin tones, since the eruption is defined by its distribution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the underlying disease is the same but the visual signal is not. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How is pityriasis rosea treated?","choices":["A tapering course of oral prednisone given over a period of two to three weeks from the outset of the eruption","Topical ketoconazole","Oral doxycycline for six weeks","It is self-limited, so reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for pruritus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ultraviolet B phototherapy or sunlight helps if begun in the first week; severe cases may be treated with acyclovir. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"Which six features are used to translate a dermatological finding into words?","choices":["Location, laterality, symmetry, tenderness, temperature and texture","Primary morphology, secondary morphology, demarcation, colour, size and distribution","Primary morphology, colour and distribution only, since the rest are subjective","Onset, duration, severity, triggers, treatments tried and response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Getting all six into a description is what lets another provider picture the lesion without seeing it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A patient with bullous pemphigoid has not responded to prednisone and doxycycline. Which agents are used for refractory disease?","choices":["Oral antihistamines and emollients while the disease burns itself out","Immunosuppressives such as methotrexate and azathioprine, with biologics and intravenous immunoglobulin also used","Ultrapotent topical corticosteroids applied to the whole of the skin surface twice daily for several weeks","Rituximab as the sole first-line agent, since it is used in pemphigus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — low-dose methotrexate is described as safe and effective in the elderly, with folic acid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 125"}]};
